Don Norman says most accidents are caused by physical limitations, which are usually well understood by designers. However, many designers fail to understand mental limitations.
He also mentions that errors usually occur when we ask people to behave in unnatural ways, like driving for long periods of time or figuring out the controls on a new stove that don't make sense.

Root Cause Analysis: Investigate the accident until the single, underlying cause is found. But, there could be mulitple layers to a problem, not just human error. But Norman believes when that is found to be a part of the problem, people often stop looking fo the deeper issues.

Norman brings up the Aifrorce with their 2010 F-22 incidents where a number of pilots were complaining about suffering from oxygen deprivation. An investigation went on, and the pilot involved in a particular crash was blamed for the incident. Pilot error was ruled as the issue, according to the Air Force, who simply did not want to or care that the Investigators found the proper ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS was sudden incapacitation due to the plane causing hypoxia.
I found his segment on how accidents can be easily blamed on human error, rather than the failure of machinery to be frustratingly funny. He mentions how when the issue is believed to be human error, we don't look into resolving any machinery issues and keep making the same mistakes in a cyclic fashion.

The 5 Whys: created by the Japanese, and used by Toyota for improving quality. When you have found one reason, keep searching for another one until you have uncovered the true underlying causes
They don't guarantee success, and different investigators could give different answers

"We can't fix problems unless people admit they exist" - pg 167
It's easier to blame people for faults, and to replace them for it too, rather than the system

Deliberate Deviations: cases where people intentionally violate procedures and regulations. This sounds like when you intentionally find glitches to exploit in a game. It's fun and challenging.
examples: going to work when sick, propping open locked doors, and driving with too little sleep

Routine Violations: occurs when non compliance is so frequent that it is ignored

Situational Violations: occurs when there are special circumstances 
example: running a red light because there's no cares around anyway and you're late. Self justifiably playing the system, like speedrunners or using AI to help finish a project you're working on the night it's due

"violations are usually causes by innapropriate rules or procedures that not only invite violation but encourage it" - pg 169
"when employees feel it
necessary to violate the rules in order to get the job done and, as a
result, succeed, they will probably be congratulated and rewarded.
This, of course, unwittingly rewards noncompliance. Cultures that
encourage and commend violations set poor role models." - page 169

Story and reflection time... This section reminds me of my time serving as a cook in the army, in Fairbanks, Alaska. I found my passion for baking and experimenting with recipes and ingredients there. Most turned out great, a few not so great. I loved baking from scratch and tweaking recipes to make new fun-looking desserts for the soldiers and they seemed to like it too. So, the military is very uptight about rules and procedures, and that included recipes as well as the amount of desserts I was tasked with putting out. There were days where my shift leaders wanted maybe 3 different types of desserts, like cookies, cake, and pie BUT I wouldn't have the ingredients to make cookies. I played around with the oil and water ratios for the boxed chocolate cake mix, formed the dough into balls, added white chocolate chips, and managed to bake them into cookies. It was like magic, and worked mysteriously too well haha. I'd do other things like add store-bought sprinkles to frosted cupcakes to make the desserts more cheery and fun-looking, or bring in matcha powder to make matcha cakes. One day a culinary SGT First Class and a colonel came in to check on the operations, they got on me for using sprinkles and playing around with recipes and ordered me to stop doing what I was doing for fear that I was either using ingredients soldiers were allergic to or potentially posioning foods. I'd gotten a reward medal from a different high-ranking official before and lots of praise otherwise for the innovative sweets, but from that day and for about a year or so on I couldn't deviate anymore and add my colorful flair to the baked goods. It made me sad, but I get it now.
On a brighter note, my confidence and daringness, i guess, to experiment with baking helped in making a night-baking prep team where I had the ability to work with the SGTs and a couple other soldiers in coming up with a solid bread roll recipe for dinner rolls, cinnamon rolls, and herbed/cheesy sandwhich breads. That was great. I guess to tie that all in to Don Norman's text, yes violations can be successfully done and non-compliance can kind of be rewarded but I would say it has to yeild positive outcomes to mean something.

Two Types of Errors: Slips and Mistakes
Human error can be divided into two major categories: slips and mistakes

Human Error: deviance from "appropriate" behavior
Slips: occurs when a person intends to do one action and ends up doing something else
-action based and memory lapse
action example: applying the correct action to the wrong object, like putting a freshly poured cup of orange juice in the refrigerator or the carton in the cabinent
memory example: forgetting to turn off the gas burner on the stove after cooking dinner, leaving the lights on or toilet seat up
often happen due to lack of attention

The three action slips most relevant to design are: capture slips, description similarity slips, mode errors
Capture Slip: instead of a desired activity, a more frequently or recently performed one gets done instead. like when pressing the wrong button for a game after swapping from a switch, xbox, or playstation controller
description-similarity slip, the error is to act upon an item similar to the target. Misclicks, wrong light switches, "does the picture look like it's function? If so, good" - me
A mode error occurs when a device has different states in which the same controls have different meanings: we call these states modes. "My air fryer does this. If I open it 5 mins early to check on an item, then close and press the play/start button. it will only run for 1 more minute despire still having 5 mins on the timer left. I have no idea how to correctly do that." - me
-mode error is really design error - pg 179

Mistake: wrong goal or wrong plan
rule based, knowledge based, memory lapse
Example of knowledge-based mistake. Weight of fuel was computed in pounds instead of kilograms.
Example of memory-lapse mistake. A mechanic failed to complete troubleshooting because of distraction
Rule-based: what worked before doesn't now because of new rulesets, pretty self explanatory, like a pokemon move or mechanic getting cut or it's attack lessened in future games 
The rest are very self explanatory and easy to understand

